nts)**? That requires all melds/pair to be 7,8,9 suited tiles. Currently:\n- Meld 1: [T6,T6,T6,T6] \u2192 T6 is *not* 7-8-9 \u2192 breaks Upper Tiles.\nSo **Upper Tiles is impossible** due to T6 kong.\n\nCould you go for **Full Flush**? All melds are in Characters or Bamboos \u2014 mixed suits. No, flush not possible.\n\nYou have a **Kong of T6**, **Chows in W6-W7-W8 and W4-W5-W6** \u2014 these overlap heavily on W6. You've already discarded W1, W2, W4, W5 \u2014 seems like cleaning up.\n\nNow consider safety and point potential.\n\nYour only chance at significant value may come from **All Pungs/Kongs (6 points)** \u2014 you have three complete melds (one kong, two chows converted?) Wait \u2014 correction: **you have one Kong [T6\u00d74]**, one Chow [W6,W7,W8], one Chow [W4,W5,W6]. But **chows are not pungs**, so you don\u2019t have four pungs/kongs yet.\n\nTo win, you need a legal hand worth \u22658 points. With chows, your best bet may be patterns like **Mixed Straight (8 points)**, which needs 123 + 456 + 789 across different suits. You have:\n- W4,W5,W6 \u2192 456\n- W6,W7,W8 \u2192 overlaps, not clean 789 (missing W9)\nBut you *have* W9 now! If you can form W7,W8,W9 as a chow, then:\n- W4,W5,W6 = 456\n- W7,W8,W9 = 789\nAnd if earlier you had a 123 somewhere\u2026 but no. Your only other meld is T6\u00d74 \u2014 not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, could you convert your current structure?\n\nWait \u2014 your **melds are fixed**. You cannot change existing melds. So your final hand will include:\n- [T6\u00d74] (kong)\n- [W6,W7,W8] (chow)\n- [W4,W5,W6] (chow)\n\u2192 That\u2019s three melds. You need one more meld and a pair.\n\nYour hand: W9, T3\u00d72, T4, F1\n\nSo you must form the fourth meld from these five tiles: W9, T3, T3, T4, F1\n\nPossible pairs: T3\u00d72 (already a pair)\n\nThen remaining: W9, T4, F1 \u2014 none can form a chow together.\n\nSo you must either:\n- Draw a tile to complete a chow or pung\n- Or use one of these as part of a chow via Chi (but only against discard of prior player)\n\nBut currently, you're about to discard.\n\nSo you must keep tiles that give you winning potential.\n\nBest candidate for completion:\n- Hold **T3\u00d72** as potential pair\n- Need one more tile to make chow with T3,T4 \u2192 e.g., T2 or T5 \u2192 T2,T3,T4 or T3,T4,T5\nBut T3,T4,x \u2192 needs T2 or T5\n\nT5 is rare: only 1 left unshown. T2: 2 left.\n\nAlternatively, **F1** \u2014 only one, hard to make pung.\n\n**W9** \u2014 alone, can only pair if draw another W9 (only 1 left).\n\nSo your most flexible path is trying to complete a **chow around T3/T4**, using either T2-T3-T4 or T3-T4-T5.\n\nYou already have T3\u00d72 and T4 \u2014 so drawing T2 or T5 would let you form a chow.\n\nBut you must discard one now.\n\nLet\u2019s evaluate safety:\n\n- **T4**: Next player just completed [T3,T4,T5] via Chi. They have already used T4. They later discarded T1, T2, B3, etc. Unlikely they need more T4. Also, only 2 T4s left unseen \u2014 one in your hand, one elsewhere. Since 2 were used in their chow, only one missing \u2014 so possibly safe.\n\n- **T3**: You have two. Total four exist. One was discarded by next player earlier? Let's check: Their history includes \"Chi T4\" \u2014 wait, they performed \"Chi T4\", meaning they used a discarded tile to complete a chow involving T4. Likely they collected T3 and T5, then chowed T4. So they had T3,T5 and chowed T4. So they only needed one T4. Thus, they probably don\u2019t have extra T3s. So T3 might be safe.\n\nBut better to keep both T3s for now, as they form a natural pair base.\n\n- **F1**: Seat wind (East). Only you and possibly others hold it. F3 (West) is prevalent wind, and someone (prev player) made a pung of F3. So West Wind is active. F1 (East) less likely to be collected. Safe to discard.\n\n- **W9**: Terminal tile. Previous player discarded W9 earlier. No one claimed. So possibly safe. But W9 could help complete a terminal chow or all terminals. But given board state, low risk.\n\nBut here\u2019s a key insight: **You already have two chows in W4-W8 range**. Adding W9 could help support **Upper Tiles** or **Big Three Winds**, but:\n- Big Three Winds requires three wind pungs \u2014 you have none except maybe F1.\nNo.\n\nBack to scoring: What patterns are still reachable?\n\nYou cannot get Full Flush (mixed suits in melds).\nCannot get All Honors.\nNot close to Seven Pairs.\n\nMost plausible:\n- **Mixed Straight (8 points)**: Needs 123, 456, 789 in different suits.\nYou already have:\n- W4,W5,W6 \u2192 456 \u2714\ufe0f\nIf you can get W7,W8,W9 \u2192 789 \u2714\ufe0f\nThen need a 123 in another suit \u2014 say T1,T2,T3 or B1,B2,B3\n\nYou have T3\u00d72, T4 \u2014 so if you draw T1,T2, could form T1,T2,T3 chow.\n\nThat would give:\n- W456, W789, T123 \u2192 Mixed Straight \u2705 (8 points)\n\nAlso, you have T6\u00d74 kong \u2014 irrelevant suit, but okay.\n\nPair: could be F1,F1 or T3,T3\n\nCurrently you have one F1 \u2014 need another.\n\nBut F1 only two left \u2014 possible.\n\nSo **Mixed Straight is feasible** if you keep T3/T4 to build toward T1-T2-T3 chow.\n\nThus, **keep T3 and T4**.\n\nTherefore, do **not** discard T3 or T4.\n\nRemaining options: **Play W9 or Play F1**\n\nBetween W9 and F1:\n- W9: Terminal, less useful for chows now (you already have high W tiles, but no way to use W9 without W7/W8 \u2014 already melded)\n- F1: Wind tile, could contribute to All Honors (unlikely), or Two Dragon Pungs (no dragons), or Prevalent/Seat Wind pattern.\n\nBut **Seat Wind (East)** gives 2 points if you have a pung of F1. You only have one. Very unlikely to get more.\n\nSo keeping F1 gives negligible advantage.\n\nMoreover, **discarding F1 is safer** than W9? Not necessarily.\n\nOpponents:\n- Prev player made F3 pung (West Wind) \u2014 prevalent wind.\n- No sign of East Wind collection.\n- Next player discarded F2 twice \u2014 so may not be collecting winds.\n- Across player played F1 once, then didn\u2019t pick up.\n\nSo F1 seems safe.\n\nW9: Only one left. Previous player discarded W9 \u2014 no claim. So likely safe too.\n\nBut W9 is a terminal \u2014 higher value in hands like All Terminals or Outside Hand.\n\nF1 is wind \u2014 also valuable, but slightly less dangerous.\n\nGiven that **Mixed Straight** is your best shot at 8 points, and it requires building a lower chow in Bamboo (T1,T2,T3), and you already have T3\u00d72, T4, you should preserve those.\n\nSo discard either W9 or F1.\n\nChoose **W9** because:\n- You already have high Character tiles melded (W4\u2013W8), but W9 doesn\u2019t extend any useful shape.\n- Holding W9 offers no further benefit.\n- F1, while weak, could marginally support wind-based scoring (2 points for Seat Wind if somehow paired, though unlikely).\n\nBut wait \u2014 **you only get Seat Wind bonus if you have a pung of F1** \u2014 single tile doesn\u2019t count. So F1 is useless unless you get three more.\n\nSo **F1 is dead weight**.\n\nSimilarly, W9 is dead.\n\nBut between two dead tiles, which is safer?\n\nF1: East Wind \u2014 less common focus. Only one J1 (dragon) used, no F1 pung seen.\n\nW9: terminal \u2014 always somewhat dangerous.\n\nBut prev player discarded W9 safely.\n\nSame for F1 \u2014 you discarded F4 earlier, no issue.\n\nBut **F1 appears less dangerous** because wind-specific strategies are rarer than terminal collections.\n\nStill, both are acceptable discards.\n\nBut recall: **Unshown tiles include only 1 W9 left**, and it hasn\u2019t been drawn yet. Same for F1 (2 unshown, one in your hand \u2192 1 elsewhere).\n\nNo strong signal.\n\nBut here's a deciding factor: **you have a kong of T6**, which is mid-game, and your chows are in W4\u2013W8.\n\nYou\u2019re likely building a **middle/lower-scoring hand**, so minimizing risk is key.\n\nSince **T3\u00d72 is a potential pair**, and T4 helps form chows, **discard W9** to remove a terminal tile and reduce outside hand risk.\n\nFinal decision: **Play W9**\n\nReason: W9 is a terminal tile with limited utility in your current hand structure. Keeping T3, T4, and F1 provides better flexibility for forming a chow in Bamboo (e.g., T1-T2-T3) to pursue Mixed Straight (8 points). Discarding W9 reduces the risk of contributing to high-value terminal hands while maintaining safer options.\n\nAnswer: Play W9"}
